# Sets and Venn Diagrams: Grouping Things Precisely

> A set is a collection with a sharp edge: either an object belongs or
> it does not — there is no "sort of". Venn diagrams turn that sharpness
> into pictures, so counting overlapping groups stops being guesswork.

## 1. What Is a Set?

[definition]
A **set** is a well-defined collection of distinct objects. Each object
is an **element** (or member), written with $\in$: if
$A = \{2, 4, 6, 8\}$ then $4 \in A$ and $5 \notin A$.
[/definition]

"Well-defined" means membership can be decided without opinion. "The
three best batsmen in the class" is *not* a set; "the students who
scored above 80" is. There are two standard ways to write a set:

| Style | Notation | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Roster form | list the elements | $A = \{2, 4, 6, 8\}$ |
| Set-builder form | state the rule | $A = \{x : x \text{ is even and } x < 10\}$ |

Both describe the same collection. Order never matters and repeats
collapse: $\{1, 2, 2, 3\}$ is just $\{1, 2, 3\}$.

## 2. Types of Sets

| Type | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Empty set $\emptyset$ | no elements | $\{x : x^2 = -1, x \text{ real}\}$ |
| Singleton | exactly one element | $\{\text{prime between } 6 \text{ and } 8\} = \{7\}$ |
| Finite | countable in principle | days of the week |
| Infinite | endless | natural numbers |
| Equal sets | exactly the same elements | $\{1,2\} = \{2,1\}$ |
| Equivalent sets | same *number* of elements | $\{a,b\}$ and $\{5,9\}$ |

Every set is a subset of itself, and $\emptyset$ is a subset of every
set — two facts that power all the counting formulas ahead.

## 3. Subsets and the Power Set

[definition]
$A$ is a **subset** of $B$ (written $A \subseteq B$) if every element
of $A$ is also in $B$. The **power set** of $A$ is the set of *all*
subsets of $A$.
[/definition]

A set with $n$ elements has $2^n$ subsets, because each element faces a
binary choice: in the subset or out. For $A = \{a, b, c\}$:

@math label="eq:st-power-set"
n(A) = 3 \;\Rightarrow\; n(P(A)) = 2^3 = 8
@/math

The eight subsets: $\emptyset$; $\{a\}$, $\{b\}$, $\{c\}$;
$\{a,b\}$, $\{a,c\}$, $\{b,c\}$; $\{a,b,c\}$. Seven are **proper**
subsets (all except $A$ itself).

[example]
How many subsets does a 5-element set have? $2^5 = 32$ — and building
them by hand is unnecessary. Each element doubles the options, which is
why the count grows so fast.
[/example]

[warning]
Distinguish a set from its elements: $\{1, 2\}$ has 2 elements, but
$\{\{1,2\}, 3\}$ has 2 elements where one *is a set*. Likewise
$\emptyset$ has no elements, yet $\{\emptyset\}$ has exactly one — the
empty set itself.
[/warning]

## 4. Set Operations

With a universe $U = \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\}$, take $A = \{1, 2, 3, 4\}$
and $B = \{3, 4, 5\}$:

| Operation | Symbol | Reads as | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Union | $A \cup B$ | in $A$ or $B$ (or both) | $\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\}$ |
| Intersection | $A \cap B$ | in both | $\{3, 4\}$ |
| Difference | $A - B$ | in $A$, not in $B$ | $\{1, 2\}$ |
| Complement | $A'$ | in $U$, not in $A$ | $\{5, 6\}$ |

@diagram tree id="st-operations" layout="tree" aria="The four set operations and what region each selects"
  "Operations on A and B" -> "Union A cup B: everything in either"
  "Operations on A and B" -> "Intersection A cap B: the overlap"
  "Operations on A and B" -> "Difference A - B: A's outer region"
  "Operations on A and B" -> "Complement A': outside A"
@/diagram

[info]
The universe $U$ is contextual. In a class survey $U$ is the class; in
number theory it might be the integers. Change $U$ and complements
change size — unions and intersections do not.
[/info]

## 5. De Morgan's Laws

Complement turns "or" into "and" and vice versa — the pair of rules
called **De Morgan's laws**:

@math label="eq:st-de-morgan"
(A \cup B)' = A' \cap B', \qquad (A \cap B)' = A' \cup B'
@/math

Verify with the sets above: $A \cup B = \{1,2,3,4,5\}$, so
$(A \cup B)' = \{6\}$. Meanwhile $A' = \{5,6\}$ and $B' = \{1,2,6\}$,
and indeed $A' \cap B' = \{6\}$. The second law checks out the same
way: $(A \cap B)' = \{3\}' = \{1,2,4,5,6\}$ and
$A' \cup B' = \{1,2,4,5,6\}$.

[tip]
In plain words: "not (A or B)" means "not A **and** not B". This is the
logic behind "no card and no cash" — failing both is failing either.
[/tip]

## 6. Counting with Two Sets

Overlaps make naive adding double-count. In a class of 40, if 25 take
mathematics and 20 take physics, the total is *not* 45 — the 8 students
taking both were counted twice. The inclusion–exclusion principle
corrects it:

@math label="eq:st-two-set"
n(A \cup B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A \cap B)
@/math

So $n(M \cup P) = 25 + 20 - 8 = 37$ take at least one subject, leaving
$40 - 37 = 3$ taking neither. The Venn picture sorts the class into
four regions: only M ($25 - 8 = 17$), only P ($20 - 8 = 12$), both
(8), neither (3).

[example]
Of 60 people, 35 like tea and 28 like coffee; 12 like both. How many
like exactly one drink? Union: $35 + 28 - 12 = 51$. Exactly one is
"union minus both": $51 - 12 = 39$. (Only tea 23 plus only coffee 16
confirms it.)
[/example]

## 7. Three-Set Venn Word Problems

Three circles create eight regions. Fill the diagram **from the centre
outwards** — the innermost count first. For sets $A$, $B$, $C$:

@math label="eq:st-three-set"
n(A \cup B \cup C) = n(A) + n(B) + n(C) - n(A \cap B) - n(A \cap C) - n(B \cap C) + n(A \cap B \cap C)
@/math

Worked survey: of 100 students, 45 play cricket (C), 38 football (F),
30 hockey (H); 12 play C and F, 10 play C and H, 8 play F and H; 4 play
all three.

1. **Centre first:** all three $= 4$.
2. **Exactly two:** $C\,F$ only $= 12 - 4 = 8$; $C\,H$ only $= 10 - 4 = 6$;
   $F\,H$ only $= 8 - 4 = 4$.
3. **Only one sport:** only C $= 45 - 8 - 6 - 4 = 27$; only F $=
   38 - 8 - 4 - 4 = 22$; only H $= 30 - 6 - 4 - 4 = 16$.
4. **Union:** $27 + 22 + 16 + 8 + 6 + 4 + 4 = 87$.
5. **Neither:** $100 - 87 = 13$.

[tip]
Start every three-set problem at the centre (all three), subtract
outwards, and finish with a full-region total check against the given
population. If the regions do not sum to the total, a number went in
twice.
[/tip]

## 8. Reading the Regions

The survey above, region by region — the "exactly two" bar is easy to
underestimate because each of its pieces is small:

@chart bar title="100 students by Venn region" labels:
Only cricket
Only football
Only hockey
Exactly two
All three
Neither

data:
27
22
16
18
4
13
@/chart

[important]
"At least two sports" $= 18 + 4 = 22$; "exactly two" $= 18$. The phrase
*at least* always pulls the all-three region back into the count —
misreading it is the most common mark-loser in Venn problems.
[/important]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Question | Tool | Answer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Subsets of an $n$-element set | power-set rule | $2^n$ |
| Proper subsets of $\{a,b,c\}$ | exclude the set itself | 7 |
| $A = \{1,2,3,4\}$, $B = \{3,4,5\}$: $A - B$ | difference | $\{1, 2\}$ |
| $(A \cup B)'$ | De Morgan | $A' \cap B'$ |
| Two-set union | add, subtract overlap | $n(A)+n(B)-n(A \cap B)$ |
| Class 40: 25 M, 20 P, 8 both | inclusion–exclusion | 37 take at least one |
| Neither subject | total minus union | 3 |
| Three-set union | subtract pairs, add centre | full formula in section 7 |
| Survey of 100: neither sport | regions sum to 87 | 13 |
| "At least two" of 18 + 4 | include the centre | 22 |
